  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Surge of warmth and humidity through Wednesday ahead \nof a cold front.\n\nA frontal zone draped to our north Tuesday will result in surface \nlow pressure riding along it Tuesday night and Wednesday. This will \npull a cold front across our area by later Wednesday. Ahead of this \nfeature, southerly flow will boost the low-level warm air advection \nand this results in much warmer air surging into our area through \nWednesday. High temperatures will near 80 degrees for many areas on \nTuesday, then into the low to even some mid 80s on Wednesday. In \naddition, dew points will increase back through the 60s Tuesday and \nWednesday, with them peaking on Wednesday (near 70F for parts of the \narea) ahead of the aforementioned front. This will result in a much \nmore humid feel over the next few days, with it being the most humid \non Wednesday (heat index values should approach 90F for areas from \nabout Philadelphia south and east; away from the coast). There does \nnot appear to be all that much in the way of forcing for ascent with \nthe front as it arrives by later Wednesday, therefore just a low \nchance (20-30 percent) for showers as of now. The dew points will \nfall from north to south later Wednesday night into Thursday as the \nfront settles southward and the winds turn out of the north and \nnortheast. The front however may not completely clear our\nregion.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front Friday brings an end to the\nwarmth as much colder air arrives for the weekend.\n\nA potent upper-level trough is forecast to arrive across the East \nduring Friday, then gradually lift out over the weekend. This will \ndrive surface low pressure just north of our area Friday with an \nassociated strong cold front sweeping through.\n\nA mild and increasingly moist air mass ahead of this feature will \nprovide some instability as the strong cold front arrives on Friday. \nLarge scale forcing for ascent will increase with the arrival of the \nrobust upper-level trough and cold front, therefore showers will \nincrease for a time (peaking on Friday). Some showers though look to \ndevelop Thursday night ahead of the main cold front. Some \nthunderstorms cannot be ruled out given the strength of the system, \nhowever this will ultimately depend on the magnitude of the \ninstability and timing of the cold front. In the wake of the cold \nfront, strong cold air advection occurs and this will result in much \ncolder temperatures for the weekend. As of now, high temperatures \nboth Saturday and Sunday look to struggle to get out of the 50s \n(colder in the higher terrain). A gusty wind Saturday due to cold \nair advection and a tightened pressure gradient will add to the \nchill. If the wind drops off enough Saturday night, some frost or \nfreezing temperatures will become a concern for portions of our \narea. The air mass then starts to modify early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Spotty minor coastal flooding will be possible \nwith the Tuesday morning high tide cycle. \n\nThe Coastal Flood Advisory expired at 5 PM for the back bay \nareas of Sussex County, DE and Ocean County, NJ (mainly Barnegat\nBay). There will be the potential for some additional minor \ncoastal flooding with Tuesday morning's high tide cycle for \nplaces along the Atlantic coast and Delaware Bay but it should \nbe more spotty in nature as we get farther way from the New \nMoon. \n\nNo tidal flooding concerns expected along the Chesapeake Bay.",
